    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. i think the price adjustment and force majeure ones are going to be the biggest points of contention. regarding the force majeure, how specific should we be? i'm worried about the supplier using vague language to get out of their obligations whenever things get difficult. how do we make sure they don't use those clauses as a loophole?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Balancing specificity in a force majeure clause is important to protect both parties without creating an unnecessarily restrictive agreement. To prevent loopholes, your clause should be:\n\n- **Specific about Covered Events:** Clearly list the events you consider 'force majeure', such as natural disasters, acts of God, wars, and government actions.\n\n- **Exclude Negligence:** State unequivocally that force majeure does not apply if the its element of force is foreseeable and within the control of the supplier.\n\n- **Include a Requirement to Mitigate:** Require the supplier to make reasonable efforts to mitigate the effects of any force majeure event.\n\n- **Set Stipulated Procedures:** Define how notice should be given and what the steps are in exploring alternative ways to fulfill contractual obligations.\n\n- **Cap on Performance:** You can include limits on the duration a force majeure event can excuse the supplier from meeting their commitments.",
      "source": "target",
